1: # Fixed-source cap-data loops: global translation comparison
2: 
3: Proved as P3K.41 after the full root audit. This note concerns the whole-zero
4: selected I1 sector of K35, with a fixed marked rational elliptic source,
5: fixed source line, fixed translation \(Q=bG\), fixed finite-root orders and
6: norm integers, and a fixed periodic index-one fan. The scalar is first
7: held fixed. A parameter family includes its actual annular maps; they
8: are never replaced merely because its individual fibres are conjugate.
9: The component-supported OS49 cusp requires a separate local lifting
10: check and is not covered by the selected-I1 argument below.
11: 
12: ## CP.1. The exact local group and its Lie sheaf
13: 
14: Let \(B\) be the orbifold \(\mathbf P^1\) with the actual cap orders. Its selected \(I_1\)
15: point is ordinary. Let \(A\) be the sheaf of fibrewise translations of the
16: compactified suspension that extend on its actual root and cusp charts.
17: All linear lattice maps are fixed. The physical delta field gives an
18: exact sequence of orbifold holomorphic vector bundles
19: 
20: \[
21:  0\longrightarrow\mathcal O_B\delta\longrightarrow V
22:        \longrightarrow L\longrightarrow0.                 \tag{CP.1}
23: \]
24: 
25: The quotient is the source translation Lie line. Here is the exact
26: coarse extension, including the changed additive root model. In the
27: minimal Weierstrass substitution
28: 
29: \[
30:  q=z^m,\qquad x=z^{2k}X,\quad y=z^{3k}Y,
31: \]
32: 
33: the source relative differential is \(z^{-k}dX/(2Y)\). Its dual source
34: translation vector therefore maps to \(z^k\) times the root translation
35: vector. The latter has deck character \(\zeta_m^k\). An invariant holomorphic
36: section has the form \(z^k h(q)\) times that root vector, because \(0\leq k<m\).
37: It is thus exactly an original source Lie section, not a different
38: coarse line. The allowed \(k\) values are \(\lfloor3m/4\rfloor\), \(\lfloor2m/3\rfloor\),
39: \(\lfloor m/4\rfloor\), \(\lfloor m/3\rfloor\), \(\lfloor m/6\rfloor\), and \(k=1\) for the quadratic I1-star
40: root. These are respectively the III-star, IV-star, III, IV, II, and
41: I1-star substitutions already proved in F41--F49. Every one has
42: \(0\leq k<m\). The root character and this equality are retained under the
43: actual line diagrams and coefficient-generic maps of F51.
44: 
45: On the selected I1 the source vector is the logarithmic Tate vector,
46: which extends as the relative dualizing vector through the actual
47: toric charts. The quotient line in (CP.1) has the original coarse
48: extension \(\mathcal O(-1)\), since the source is rational with \(\chi(\mathcal O)=1\). The
49: physical delta line has coarse extension \(\mathcal O\). Taking finite invariants
50: is exact over \(\mathbb C\). Hence the coarse direct image of \(V\) is an extension
51: of \(\mathcal O(-1)\) by \(\mathcal O\), and the elementary two-chart Laurent calculation gives
52: 
53: \[
54:                        H^1(B,V)=0.                         \tag{CP.2}
55: \]
56: 
57: At a reduced retained/root fibre the source component group can be
58: removed from a translation lift by a suspension power: the actual
59: source translation \(Q\) acts transitively on those components, by the
60: already proved sphere restriction K15. The remaining central point
61: lies in the additive or multiplicative identity group and has a local
62: logarithm. On a good root ordinary elliptic local logarithms apply.
63: At the selected I1 cusp the index-one component lattice gives the
64: unique integral toric reindexing making an extending translation
65: preserve the chosen component; the remaining two torus multipliers
66: are holomorphic units. Their logarithms give the local Lie lift.
67: This is the actual local argument of P5.5, which uses the toric cover
68: and its deck lattice, not a claim based on the dimension of an
69: automorphism group. In particular
70: 
71: \[
72:  0\longrightarrow H\longrightarrow V\xrightarrow{\exp}A
73:        \longrightarrow0                                   \tag{CP.3}
74: \]
75: 
76: is exact as a sequence of sheaves on the orbifold atlas and its
77: full groupoid, where \(H\) is the extending integral period sheaf. It
78: is not an assertion that exponentiation is surjective on invariant
79: sections of a coarse root disc. A fixed torsion translation can lack
80: an invariant Lie logarithm; its non-equivariant atlas logarithm
81: has a finite-group lattice discrepancy, which remains in (CP.3).
82: Taking finite invariants was used only for the complex vector sheaf
83: \(V\) in (CP.1)--(CP.2), where averaging is exact.
84: 
85: ## CP.2. Parametric conjugacies and the discrete torsor class
86: 
87: Work first over a small simply connected parameter disc. F44 and F49
88: give actual local source conjugations, with the line lifts and their
89: transported annular maps. Their constructions are analytic in that
90: parameter after shrinking: a central isogeny has a local inverse at
91: each chosen inverse image; the multiplicative square root has a local
92: analytic branch; the residual zero-central logarithm is solved by the
93: displayed finite averaging sum. The residual line unit has its
94: central finite character retained and its zero-central logarithm
95: solved by the same sum. The component choices and this finite
96: character are locally constant. No choice has yet been made around
97: the entire parameter circle.
98: 
99: At the cusp the scalar and the source line are fixed, so the same
100: toric translation model is used. Comparing all these local models
101: with one fixed member gives a cocycle in \(A\) on the full Cech
102: groupoid of the actual orbifold atlas. This includes the finite-root
103: group cochains as well as ordinary overlaps. Its image under (CP.3)
104: is a lattice-valued total two-cocycle. Local atlas logarithms can be
105: chosen continuously on the parameter disc; they are not presumed
106: invariant. Their finite-group discrepancies and ordinary overlap
107: discrepancies are continuous lattice-valued functions and hence are
108: locally constant. Subtract the reference-parameter cocycle and use
109: the corresponding integral total-cochain adjustment. The result is
110: a \(V\)-valued total one-cocycle, including its finite-group terms.
111: Equation (CP.2) solves this cocycle; finite averaging is valid here
112: because \(V\) is a complex vector sheaf. Thus no fixed torsion point is
113: removed by an unjustified invariant-logarithm choice.
114: 
115: This solution can be taken analytic in the parameter. On the two
116: standard source-base charts, the solution is the Cauchy/Laurent
117: splitting of the negative and nonnegative powers for \(\mathcal O\) and \(\mathcal O(-1)\);
118: finite averaging gives its orbifold version. The integrals are over
119: fixed compact circles, so holomorphic parameter dependence is
120: preserved. A splitting of the vector extension in (CP.1) also exists,
121: since its extension group has coarse \(H^1(\mathcal O(1))=0\); the same Laurent
122: calculation constructs it. Exponentiating the resulting sections
123: gives the actual global family isomorphism. On every local chart it
124: is the specified extending translation, so it transports the
125: annular, finite-character, line, and cusp maps literally.
126: 
127: Consequently a fixed-scalar cap-data family is locally isomorphic to
128: the fixed compactified suspension by translations. Continuing these
129: actual isomorphisms around a parameter interval leaves a global
130: extending translation at the endpoint. The finite kernel of \(1-D\)
131: has not been discarded: it is included in that endpoint. The next
132: step computes this global endpoint group.
133: 
134: ## CP.3. Lifting an endpoint away from the selected I1
135: 
136: Put \(U=\mathbf P^1\setminus\{c\}\), where \(c\) is the selected
137: I1. We give the actual local lift, including at every reduced root or
138: retained fibre. On such a root disc the construction is literally
139: \[
140:               X'=N^\times/\langle B_\lambda\rangle .
141:                                                                \tag{CP.3a}
142: \]
143: Here \(N\) is the actual corrected multidegree-zero root line, and
144: \(B_\lambda\) is an invertible line map with strict uniform norm
145: contraction. Thus (CP.3a) is an unramified regular covering of smooth
146: total spaces. The same description holds at every uncapped retained
147: fibre, since the only zero of the Hom section is at \(c\).
148: 
149: Choose a holomorphic nonzero vector \(v_O(z)\) in \(N|_O\), possible
150: on this disc. For an extending translation \(\tau\), the map
151: \(z\mapsto\tau([v_O(z)])\) is an actual holomorphic section of
152: \(X'\). Pulling the covering (CP.3a) back to the simply connected
153: root disc gives a trivial covering; lifting this one section gives
154: a holomorphic map \(p(z)\in N^\times\). Its projection \(T(z)\)
155: to the source extends through \(z=0\). It meets the smooth locus
156: of the reduced central fibre: \([v_O(0)]\) is a regular point of
157: the map to the disc, a base-preserving automorphism preserves this
158: property, and the quotient covering is locally biholomorphic. For
159: the good root this assertion is immediate. For reduced II, III,
160: IV or \(I_n\) the regular locus of that map is exactly the smooth locus
161: of the reduced fibre. Compose the lifted point with a suspension
162: power if necessary to put its central component in the identity
163: component. The required power exists because the actual Q-action
164: is transitive on the components. This is a definite deck map in
165: (CP.3a), not an identification of distinct central components by hand.
166: 
167: The resulting section translation \(t_T\) has an invertible line
168: lift \(L_T\) on the entire root surface. To see the extension,
169: \(t_T^*N\otimes N^{-1}\) is generically trivial. Proper direct
170: image gives a generator whose evaluation divisor is vertical. Its
171: intersection with every component is zero, since both lines have
172: multidegree zero. The explicit component intersection matrix has
173: primitive kernel the whole reduced fibre. Dividing by the
174: corresponding integral power of its base equation therefore leaves
175: a nowhere-zero section. This is the actual construction of \(L_T\)
176: and applies equally to the II, III, IV, \(I_2\) and retained \(I_n\) charts.
177: For a good fibre the same argument has its single reduced component.
178: The finite quotient/resolution and transported-centre diagrams are
179: those of F41--F49; the line being used here is their actual N.
180: 
181: There is exact commutation with the suspension, including its line
182: scalar. On a smooth generic source elliptic fibre write the
183: specified degree-zero line as
184: \[
185:  (\mathbf C\times\mathbf C^*)/\Lambda,\qquad
186:  (z,v)\sim(z+\omega,\chi(\omega)v).
187: \]
188: The constant factors of automorphy give a literal lift of a source
189: translation, \((z,v)\mapsto(z+t,av)\). Every other holomorphic
190: line lift of that translation differs by a nowhere-zero holomorphic
191: function on the compact elliptic curve, hence by a constant. Thus
192: any two such lifts commute. In particular \([L_T,B_\lambda]=1\)
193: on every generic fibre, and holomorphic extension gives equality
194: on the entire reduced/root chart. Compare \(L_T(v_O)\) with the
195: chosen lifted point \(p\). Their ratio is a holomorphic base unit
196: \(c(z)\). The actual map \(c(z)L_T\) therefore has the same value
197: as \(\tau\) at the chosen origin of every regular compact torus.
198: Two translations with the same origin value agree. Equality on the
199: dense regular locus proves that \(c(z)L_T\) is an extending lift of
200: \(\tau\) on all of (CP.3a). This constructs the lift without
201: assuming a covering-lift theorem for an arbitrary singular fibre.
202: 
203: It is exactly equivariant under the finite cap action. Pullback of
204: a base-preserving automorphism to the actual root cover is defined
205: by \((x,z)\mapsto(\tau x,z)\); it commutes with its deck action.
206: The two line-cover maps \(A_i L A_i^{-1}\) and \(L\) therefore
207: differ by \(B_\lambda^{j_i}\), where \(j_i\) is an integer constant
208: on the connected root disc. Iterating \(m_i\) times gives
209: \(B_\lambda^{m_i j_i}\). But \(A_i^{m_i}=B_\lambda^{n_i}\),
210: and the just-proved commutation with \(B_\lambda\) makes this
211: iterate the identity. Strict contraction on the covering line forces
212: \(j_i=0\). The lift is consequently exactly equivariant for the
213: actual affine action; its component and finite line character have
214: not been omitted.
215: 
216: The overlaps of these lifts differ by suspension powers and give
217: an integral one-cocycle on \(U\) with its finite orbifold points.
218: Its cohomology is zero. Explicitly the orbifold fundamental group
219: is the free product of the finite cyclic cap groups, and every
220: homomorphism from it to \(\mathbf Z\) is zero. Changing the local
221: lifts by the resulting integral powers of \(B_\lambda\) patches
222: them to one lift on \(U\). Exact finite-root equivariance descends
223: its source point to an original source section. The actual
224: root/quotient/resolution diagrams give its meromorphic line map
225: through the original additive fibre. On an overlap the scalar
226: ratio is a base function, since it is constant on the compact
227: smooth source elliptic fibre.
228: 
229: Here is the selected-cusp comparison, with both integer reindexings
230: written. In its actual Tate and line coordinates the two period maps
231: have the form
232: \[
233:  \begin{split}
234:  h:(Z,V)&\longmapsto(q_T Z,P_T(q)V),\\
235:  B_\lambda:(Z,V)&\longmapsto(Q_T(q)Z,
236:                               q\lambda C(q)V),
237:  \end{split}                                                \tag{CP.3b}
238: \]
239: where \(q_T/q,P_T,Q_T,C\) are holomorphic units. The source
240: sections and the line divisor sections avoid the selected node;
241: this gives the asserted unit Tate coordinates. In the height-one
242: component lattice the period shifts of (CP.3b) are exactly
243: \((1,0)\) and \((0,1)\). This is the whole-zero I1, index-one
244: calculation, and is why the component-supported OS49 case is kept
245: outside this note.
246: 
247: An extending translation on the toric covering sends its chosen
248: component to the component with a definite index \((r,s)\).
249: It induces the identity on the nearby marked lattice, hence commutes
250: with the component deck action. Compose its lift with
251: \(h^{-r}B_\lambda^{-s}\). It now preserves the chosen component
252: and each of its boundary strata. On the open torus times the base
253: its map is literally
254: \[
255:                   (Z,V,q)\longmapsto(a(q)Z,b(q)V,q).
256:                                                                \tag{CP.3c}
257: \]
258: Evaluate it and its inverse at \((Z,V)=(1,1)\). Since both preserve
259: the open central torus, \(a\) and \(b\) are holomorphic units
260: at zero. This proves the bounded multiplier and its inverse, not
